#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "17b77faa-ab56-5302-bc25-3cc7152243c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13020" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{6} & =(1+0.15)^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(0.15)^{1}+15(0.15)^{2}+20(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+6(0.15)+15(0.022\\,5)+20(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+0.9+0.337\\,5+0.067\\,5 \\\\  & =2.305,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "c284dfb0-75ca-5f48-b44b-c2ce5ea37d4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13021" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{8} & =1+8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+8x+28{x}^{2}+56{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.89)^{8} & =(1+(-0.11))^{8} \\\\  & ≈1+8(-0.11)^{1}+28(-0.11)^{2}+56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1-8(0.11)+28(0.012\\,1)-56(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1-0.88+0.338\\,8-0.074\\,536 \\\\  & =0.384\\,264,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "f8a06578-35af-5510-90ca-8ca2f8c8203f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13022" :question/problem "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left( 2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{4}= & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(2)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(2)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+4{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(2)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =16+4(8)(x)+6(4)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =16+32x+24{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.97)^{4} & =(2-0.03)^{4} \\\\  & ≈16+32(-0.03)+24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =16-0.96+0.021\\,6 \\\\  & =15.0616\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "461efa2a-0fd5-5a28-843d-422a56fbf031" :question/math-academy-id "e-13023" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\ldots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1,$ $b = x,$ and $n = 5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(1)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(1)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(1)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(1)}^{5}+5{(1)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(1)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(1)(x)+10(1)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.08)^{5} & =(1+0.08)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.08)+10(0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.08)+10(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.4+0.064 \\\\  & =1.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.08)^{5}-1.464|}{(1.08)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.36%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efb9bc46-3c46-5225-a62e-db389171615e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341467} {:answer/id #uuid "5f062ea8-86a1-5242-b729-54e6c00b860d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341468} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc0e3b9-37ed-5d8c-bbae-ec20fceb291b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341469} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe285bf-8b87-56a0-8a76-2b48ebdc4038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341470} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ff5825-c31d-5122-927d-b26adebceaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.07%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfe285bf-8b87-56a0-8a76-2b48ebdc4038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341470} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd76774-8dfe-558b-add5-84c24403f423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca6e7f72-b383-5353-a2b5-6242ed2f3e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-134531" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.02)^{7}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f586d-8328-53dd-9f2f-83841c2d7983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341474} {:answer/id #uuid "0736794e-9308-5b63-b1b8-a4c92b2840c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.10%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341475} {:answer/id #uuid "756d4602-b8b3-5195-bd0f-149def339e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.07%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341476} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf8d9a-b289-561f-94f3-4d1d1a23caec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341477} {:answer/id #uuid "30a197bd-b0fc-5cd8-870b-e81ac5b79265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0736794e-9308-5b63-b1b8-a4c92b2840c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.10%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341475} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c8ae942-23ef-5701-9bcc-d0e8ecdb8de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "201de3ad-c6dc-5dd3-8566-2c0956be1132" :question/math-academy-id "q-134533" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.1)^{5} & =(2+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(0.1)+80(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =32+80(0.1)+80(0.01) \\\\  & =32+8+0.8 \\\\  & =40.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "823a093b-e4b6-5da4-bd39-c3a5a2a835da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341481} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8995b2-9908-55d7-af8c-14cdc678f6e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341482} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae461e-8cf9-5b86-a3f9-7b2d1e8d55bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341483} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4dd1d3-a6e0-57a2-a7bb-fbbb58a14e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341484} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ed37a6-cfa2-5a8b-ae43-b0804a7b546e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6ed37a6-cfa2-5a8b-ae43-b0804a7b546e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341485} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c01c720-c314-51be-8b80-cc2f2c173445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ba96c61-c614-58f4-9cbe-00016a202776" :question/math-academy-id "q-134534" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.98)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{5} & =(2+(-0.02))^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(-0.02)+80(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =32-80(0.02)+80(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =32-1.6+0.032 \\\\  & =30.432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.98)^{5}-30.432|}{(1.98)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.0%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5d87437-0af1-5704-b0ea-dd4ec036a9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.00%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341488} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f4ca61-a0c9-55b5-9ef3-6a34e1acca0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341489} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebba384-09db-597a-bcd0-0479fd5961a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341490} {:answer/id #uuid "66211a9e-8213-5add-ad54-ed4118292a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341491} {:answer/id #uuid "f658969d-a0ed-5e73-94e7-d289e61198e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f658969d-a0ed-5e73-94e7-d289e61198e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341492} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d8463a-17d8-5334-884e-e816df59abbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e76bf69-0203-5fbd-82f9-53054708bcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-134536" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.92)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc676e4-d01f-545d-a74d-7927ac992b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.142\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341495} {:answer/id #uuid "95979821-7d0f-5285-8126-23dafa1b0bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.210\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341496} {:answer/id #uuid "6baaeb36-9b85-5b63-85d9-91ecb2e9cbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.164\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341497} {:answer/id #uuid "dea13688-fd6a-5c8b-86a3-51964879c2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.149\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341498} {:answer/id #uuid "c4409990-bc30-5f14-9601-cb03723c91c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.145\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc676e4-d01f-545d-a74d-7927ac992b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.142\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341495} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6c2e4b-993f-5692-a489-ea347576046e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42cfaebd-c61b-5499-96a6-a22612d8275f" :question/math-academy-id "q-134606" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.12)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.12$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.12)^{7} & =(1+0.12)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.12)+21(0.12)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.12)+21(0.014\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.84+0.302\\,4 \\\\  & =2.142\\,4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf5daa27-6c43-5f6f-95bc-726e7f8e6bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.736" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341502} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3cb5d9-2595-5710-938f-5434b221ef26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.739" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341503} {:answer/id #uuid "ec02dfd2-7265-56c6-878d-ae93d5126942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341504} {:answer/id #uuid "27b8f105-0fe9-5f7e-b3ed-4cc6f01f81c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341505} {:answer/id #uuid "302e8508-562a-5d6c-bdce-289f9e321505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.734" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf5daa27-6c43-5f6f-95bc-726e7f8e6bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.736" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341502} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1623000-4304-5d1c-9369-ae4ad786a360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "731be3b4-2a40-528f-bb62-10cc1a8d179e" :question/math-academy-id "q-134609" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.94)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.06$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.94)^{6} & =(1+(-0.06))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(-0.06)+10(-0.06)^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(0.06)+10(0.003\\,6) \\\\  & =1-0.3+0.036 \\\\  & =0.736,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac1f8b9-dad8-5bf1-a6b0-41ce2b71c1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097981} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fb25be-1a4b-5f4b-bdf8-ea2e28990a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097982} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc65b54-38af-5482-8754-76ee16042c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097983} {:answer/id #uuid "482c65b8-c365-520b-88af-07de59d3a228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097984} {:answer/id #uuid "67f4bba5-5b79-57da-903d-8608b5fc2f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.046" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482c65b8-c365-520b-88af-07de59d3a228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "269.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097984} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ac6d83-23ee-5688-8443-4032786cf965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186097980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46b2aad9-462e-5a2f-8a13-0ac0cbf2c755" :question/math-academy-id "q-134630" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(4+x)^{4}≈256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.05$ into the expansion of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.05)^{4} & =(4+0.05)^{4} \\\\  & ≈256+256(0.05)+96(0.05)^{2} \\\\  & =256+256(0.05)+96(0.002\\,5) \\\\  & =256+12.8+0.24 \\\\  & =269.040,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce9f392a-11bc-5ac6-bb74-2880202e43fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.832\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341509} {:answer/id #uuid "b02eacde-d6b2-54f4-8b9b-035a832b26e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.072\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341510} {:answer/id #uuid "9128cf0f-e79a-5e5f-8ad2-91379152d7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.067\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341511} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddea8eb-c315-5494-abeb-19e42c532aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.067\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341512} {:answer/id #uuid "c262e206-baad-57c0-8b9e-d005dd96d38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.932\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c262e206-baad-57c0-8b9e-d005dd96d38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.932\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341513} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f6a5ff-ce33-542f-99e3-10e481b6a62e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dd05574-640c-5b9b-a9a5-c23bbe5e5973" :question/math-academy-id "q-178288" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.99)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.99)^{7} & =(1+(-0.01))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.01)+21(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =1-7(0.01)+21(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =1-0.07+0.002\\,1 \\\\  & =0.932\\,1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bcb748-4216-5cfc-827f-12f0db8611fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.753\\,60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341516} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbcc5f7-ba4e-5299-97f1-daa48539c70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.248\\,64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341517} {:answer/id #uuid "bde8833b-57d3-59da-b0a1-b93266680f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.751\\,36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341518} {:answer/id #uuid "d7059860-768c-52ee-9600-97c54d3db95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.315\\,84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341519} {:answer/id #uuid "962ffc73-4f0c-5c52-9f37-209eb2019402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.751\\,47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde8833b-57d3-59da-b0a1-b93266680f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.751\\,36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341518} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c437d6-3d88-50d0-b5c7-bae09539f9b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70cb9087-035f-5609-8f85-8a97f97e6d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-178291" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.96)^{7}.$ Give your answer to five decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.96)^{7} & =(1+(-0.04))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.04)+21(-0.04)^{2}+35(-0.04)^{3} \\\\  & =1-7(0.04)+21(0.001\\,6)-35(0.000\\,064) \\\\  & =1-0.28+0.033\\,6-0.002\\,24 \\\\  & =0.751\\,36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1b2a2a7-0975-5e41-a146-b457e9f896ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341523} {:answer/id #uuid "60002b58-c106-5017-ac5d-24f1597d89e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.589" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341524} {:answer/id #uuid "763043e5-75e7-52b1-8393-3f64c9279ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341525} {:answer/id #uuid "85c4218d-0c1e-5c6b-9751-d91cc0fd20b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.449" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341526} {:answer/id #uuid "45bc3e27-0c18-5729-8ebf-45d041f02d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45bc3e27-0c18-5729-8ebf-45d041f02d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341527} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ced7d05-d001-504d-a4ea-3b543c3c491a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68e86c9d-f777-5ade-ba76-e4af97e240d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-178357" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(3.99)^{4}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(4+x)}^{4}≈256+256x+96{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.99)^{4} & =(4+(-0.01))^{4} \\\\  & ≈256+256(-0.01)+96(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =256-256(0.01)+96(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =256-2.56+0.009\\,6 \\\\  & =253.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94bf142a-a464-5f2b-808e-d86c861c4c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098969} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e34cc7-9db5-51e7-87d4-c4852be6632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098970} {:answer/id #uuid "faa2495f-b8f0-5218-800a-5d6ae47b0395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098971} {:answer/id #uuid "27a0fc26-e591-5986-a6f7-a3142271bad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098972} {:answer/id #uuid "78763fb8-3665-5826-b3c7-85e2c8ca2d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e34cc7-9db5-51e7-87d4-c4852be6632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098970} :answer-field/id #uuid "925d0b70-740d-5e36-89fa-932179e60f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fb221ee-2793-577c-8730-703585d0f6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-18703" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.2$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.2)^{5} & =(3+0.2)^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(0.2)+270(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =243+405(0.2)+270(0.0\\,4) \\\\  & =243+81+10.8 \\\\  & =334.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(3.2)^{5}-334.8|}{(3.2)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1bd1905-a16f-5c4d-afe7-20f75b873d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.311" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1bd1905-a16f-5c4d-afe7-20f75b873d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.311" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341530} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96872d4-0c47-5893-b636-739d3c55ab33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2acc6a9-7b45-5e9c-933e-9d9185882e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-234172" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.19)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.19)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.19$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.19)^{5} & =(1+0.19)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.19)+10(0.19)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.19)+10(0.036\\,1) \\\\  & =1+0.95+0.361 \\\\  & ≈2.311,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623471dd-99ad-564b-9d25-2203e6114e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74902.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232850} {:answer/id #uuid "89e02f71-b91a-57b4-9eba-ee015857305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74902.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "623471dd-99ad-564b-9d25-2203e6114e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74902.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232850} :answer-field/id #uuid "6905f865-4d9b-5cc9-b985-cdd50bb03d27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b15cfa5-4c3c-5ada-8df5-1143a4805595" :question/math-academy-id "q-235253" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$(4.97)^{7}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=5,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5+x)}^{7} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(5)}^{7}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(5)}^{7-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(5)}^{7-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(5)}^{7}+7{(5)}^{6}{(x)}^{1}+21{(5)}^{5}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =78\\,125+7(15\\,625)(x)+21(3\\,125)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =78\\,125+109\\,375x+65\\,625{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(5+x)}^{7}≈78\\,125+109\\,375x+65\\,625{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the above approximation of $(5+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.97)^{7} & =(5+(-0.03))^{7} \\\\  & ≈78\\,125+109\\,375(-0.03)+65\\,625(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =78\\,125+109\\,375(-0.03)+65\\,625(0.000\\,9) \\\\  & =78\\,125-3\\,281.25+59.062\\,5 \\\\  & ≈74\\,902.81,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7a03bac-6486-5ef0-ae9b-6fd88e930e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a03bac-6486-5ef0-ae9b-6fd88e930e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341534} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ed2190-44d1-5859-bc40-7410b15670e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da651534-8aa1-5039-a429-faf324f356de" :question/math-academy-id "q-265895" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.05)^{7}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.05)^{7}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.05$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.05)^{7} & =(1+0.05)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.05)+21(0.05)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.05)+21(0.002\\,5) \\\\  & =1+0.35+0.052\\,5 \\\\  & ≈1.403,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f86b6293-38e8-5dc7-9ecf-b88b3893069d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341537} {:answer/id #uuid "218ff919-ebeb-5976-90b9-1a85fcbeea81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f86b6293-38e8-5dc7-9ecf-b88b3893069d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341537} :answer-field/id #uuid "e91eb2f8-2523-5a71-b0d0-7702eca12957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afaecf66-0759-5896-86a8-e21ce74eae49" :question/math-academy-id "q-265899" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^{10}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places.\n\n$(1.15)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{10} & =(1+0.15)^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(0.15)+45(0.15)^{2}+120(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+10(0.15)+45(0.022\\,5)+120(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+1.5+1.012\\,5+0.405 \\\\  & ≈3.917\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d607d054-7ee7-51cf-9821-c566448172c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.685" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341541} {:answer/id #uuid "951c092a-d97b-532c-9924-2b58544b29ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.684" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951c092a-d97b-532c-9924-2b58544b29ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.684" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341542} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b572bc-1860-5db6-8997-593c9fd29842" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fea4a9c1-07dd-51cd-a1e0-aa68ec9df6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-265990" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.11)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.11)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.11)^{5} & =(1+0.11)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.11)+10(0.11)^{2}+10(0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1+5(0.11)+10(0.012\\,1)+10(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1+0.55+0.121+0.013\\,31 \\\\  & ≈1.684,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eff7c4d-b3dd-5262-97ed-82de2c1a8719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.571" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eff7c4d-b3dd-5262-97ed-82de2c1a8719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.571" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341545} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae2a0d44-4606-5140-b4a5-c1e733ef82fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "061c567f-edc2-5ea9-bca1-bf6e8bfbfa7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-265992" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(0.89)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.89)^{5} & =(1+(-0.11))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(-0.11)+10(-0.11)^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(0.11)+10(0.012\\,1) \\\\  & =1-0.55+0.121 \\\\  & ≈0.571,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d473507-1126-5340-b32f-376c78d018c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d473507-1126-5340-b32f-376c78d018c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341548} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae231fd2-23f8-5f50-80aa-35bfc31a8cb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "381392d2-7805-5631-8ab9-37792d208dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-266001" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.84)^{10}.$ Round your answer to five decimal places.\n\n$(0.84)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.16$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.84)^{10} & =(1+(-0.16))^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(-0.16)+45(-0.16)^{2}+120(-0.16)^{3} \\\\  & =1-10(0.16)+45(0.025\\,6)-120(0.004\\,096) \\\\  & =1-1.6+1.152-0.491\\,52 \\\\  & ≈0.060\\,48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d25215eb-25a0-5a48-b4e4-2dbf1051f89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "889.396" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341551} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f74e46-756c-5109-9ecf-038e32a7948c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "889.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f74e46-756c-5109-9ecf-038e32a7948c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "889.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341552} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32c4d8f-1733-5838-b609-ea37a9ad9785" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cb71077-9dfd-515e-9c32-f7d8274dbcc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-266342" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{9}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(2.13)^{9}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(2.13)^{9}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=9,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{9} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{0})(2)^{9}(x)^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{1})(2)^{9-1}(x)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{2})(2)^{9-2}(x)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(2)^{9}+9(2)^{8}(x)^{1}+36(2)^{7}(x)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =512+9(256)x+36(128){x}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =512+2\\,304x+4\\,608{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(2+x)^{9}≈512+2\\,304x+4\\,608{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.13$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^{9}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.13)^{9} & =(2+0.13)^{9} \\\\  & ≈512+2\\,304(0.13)+4\\,608(0.13)^{2} \\\\  & =512+2\\,304(0.13)+4\\,608(0.016\\,9) \\\\  & =512+299.52+77.875\\,2 \\\\  & ≈889.395,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb4a3da7-59d3-572e-97b5-7c74e9503e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "467.763" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4a3da7-59d3-572e-97b5-7c74e9503e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "467.763" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341555} :answer-field/id #uuid "664cc87a-171e-5ada-8bb8-84b8d2a265fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed0ff3ef-efc5-525f-8553-028e37ec293e" :question/math-academy-id "q-266355" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{9}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.98)^{9}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.98)^{9}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=9,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{9} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{0})(2)^{9}(x)^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{1})(2)^{9-1}(x)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{2})(2)^{9-2}(x)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(2)^{9}+9(2)^{8}x+36(2)^{7}{x}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =512+9(256)x+36(128){x}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =512+2\\,304x+4\\,608{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(2+x)^{9}≈512+2\\,304x+4\\,608{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{9}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{9} & =(2+(-0.02))^{9} \\\\  & ≈512+2\\,304(-0.02)+4\\,608(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =512+2\\,304(-0.02)+4\\,608(0.0004) \\\\  & =512-46.08+1.843\\,2 \\\\  & ≈467.763,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}]}
